  traps:[{ t:"A property purchase by a resident non-national is exactly what a cross-border rule surfaces, and here two thirds of it is evidenced to the euro from audited accounts. That part of the case is finished." },
    { t:"Residence-by-investment programmes are lawful. Participating in one is not an indicator; it raises the standard of evidence on source of funds." },
    { t:"Family lending is ordinary. The gap is that the lender's own funding is unevidenced, not that the loan exists." }],
  typology:["Unevidenced source of funds on part of a transaction","Related-party lending"],
  explanationSummary:"The customer explains the purchase as funded by a company dividend and a secured loan from a relative's company, and produces documentation for both.",
  assessment:"Two thirds of the funding is evidenced to a standard that would satisfy any review. The remaining EUR 380,000 rests on a related-party loan whose lender's own source of funds is unknown — a specific, closable gap rather than a basis for suspicion.",
  residual:"obtain the lender's audited accounts or bank statements evidencing its own funding, and record the residence-by-investment context on the customer risk assessment.",
  keyReq:["prior","source3","anatolia","residency","explain","proof"],
  hints:["Split the funding into its components and establish how much of it is actually evidenced before forming a view on the whole.",
    "The second ordering party is the open question. What would you need from it, and could the customer realistically obtain that?",
    "Check whether the elevated-risk feature here is the customer's conduct or the route she lawfully used."],
